-- title:
--   Weakly regular sequences on a nonzero free module
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $M$ be an $R$-module (an additive commutative group with an $R$-module structure) which is free over $R$ and nontrivial, i.e. $M \neq 0$; both $R$ and $M$ may lie in arbitrary, unrelated universes. Let $s$ be a list $(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ of elements of $R$. The assertion is the equivalence of two instances of Mathlib's weak-regularity predicate for sequences: $s$ is a weakly regular sequence on $M$ — that is, for each index $i$, multiplication by $x_i$ is injective on the quotient $M/(x_1,\dots,x_{i-1})M$ by the submodule generated by the earlier members of the list — if and only if $s$ is a weakly regular sequence on $R$ regarded as a module over itself, i.e. for each $i$ multiplication by $x_i$ is injective on $R/(x_1,\dots,x_{i-1})$. No finiteness is imposed on the rank of $M$ or on the length of $s$, and no hypothesis on $R$ beyond commutativity.
--
--   This is the standard fact that a nonzero free module detects exactly the weakly regular sequences of the base ring, whence a nonzero free module has the same depth behaviour as the ring itself; it underlies the statement that a free module over a regular local ring attains maximal depth, as used in the Auslander–Buchsbaum step of Taylor–Wiles–Kisin patching. Within the development it is invoked in the proof that a regular local ring is a unique factorisation domain, [`IsRegularRing.uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalRing`](thm.html#IsRegularRing.uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalRing).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_RingTheory_Sequence_isWeaklyRegular_of_free.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
